<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>2.5.3: Solving Systems of Linear Equations in Two Variables</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00436</md:content-id>
    <md:title>2.5.3: Solving Systems of Linear Equations in Two Variables</md:title>
    <md:uuid>7d8fe57a-6bf8-493e-bf91-50723a48bd56</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity (10 minutes)</title>
<para>In this activity, students practice using algebra to solve systems of linear equations in two variables and checking their solutions. Students do not have to use elimination, but the equations in the first two systems conveniently have opposites for the coefficients of one variable, so one variable can be easily eliminated.</para>
<para>In the last two systems, none of the coefficients of the variables are opposites. Some students may choose to solve the systems by substitution, but the process would be pretty cumbersome. The complication students encounter here motivates the need for another move, which students will explore in the next lesson.</para>
<para>Students who opt to use technology to check their solutions practice choosing tools strategically.</para>
<title>Launch</title>
<para>Keep students in groups of 2 and provide continued access to graphing technology, in case students need it for checking solutions.</para>
<para>If time is limited, ask one partner in each group to solve the first two systems and the other partner to solve the last two, and then ask partners to check each other’s solutions.</para>
<title>Student Activity</title>
<para>Solve each system of equations without graphing, show your reasoning, and check your solutions.</para>
<para>System A</para>
<para>\(\left\{\begin{array}{c}2x+3y=7\\−2x+4y=14\end{array}\right.\)</para>
<para>Use System A to answer questions 1–3.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item> What is the value of \(x\)? </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> -1</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item> What is the value of \(y\)? </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 3</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item> Show your reasoning and check the solution. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis></para>
<para> \(\begin{array}{l}\underline{\begin{array}{rcl}2x+3y&amp;=&amp;7\\+\;\;\;\;\;\;-2x+4y&amp;=&amp;14\end{array}}\\\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;7y=21\\\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;y=3\end{array}\)</para>

<para> \(\begin{array}{rcl}2x+3y&amp;=&amp;7\\2x+3(3)&amp;=&amp;7\\2x+9&amp;=&amp;7\\2x&amp;=&amp;-2\\x&amp;=&amp;-1\end{array}\)</para>

<para> \(\begin{array}{rcl}2x+3y&amp;=&amp;7\\2(-1)+3(3)&amp;=&amp;7\\-2+9&amp;=&amp;7\\7&amp;=&amp;7\checkmark\end{array}\)</para>

<para> \(\begin{array}{rcl}-2x+4y&amp;=&amp;14\\-2(-1)+4(3)&amp;=&amp;14\\2+12&amp;=&amp;14\\14&amp;=&amp;14\checkmark\end{array}\)</para>
<para>System B</para>
<para>\(\left\{\begin{array}{c}2x+3y=7\\3x−3y=3\end{array}\right.\)</para>
<para>Use System B to answer questions 4–6.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item> What is the value of \(x\)? </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 2</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item> What is the value of \(y\)? </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 1</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="6" type="enumerated">
  <item> Show your reasoning and check the solution. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis></para>
<para>\(\begin{array}{l}\underline{\begin{array}{rcl}2x+3y&amp;=&amp;7\\+\;\;\;\;\;3x-3y&amp;=&amp;3\end{array}}\\\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;5x=10\\\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;x=2\end{array}\)</para>

<para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}2x+3y&amp;=&amp;7\\2(2)+3y&amp;=&amp;7\\4+3y&amp;=&amp;7\\3y&amp;=&amp;3\\y&amp;=&amp;1\end{array}\)</para>

<para> \(\begin{array}{rcl}3x-3y&amp;=&amp;3\\3(2)-3(1)&amp;=&amp;3\\6-3&amp;=&amp;3\\3&amp;=&amp;3\checkmark\end{array}\)</para>

<para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}2x+3y&amp;=&amp;7\\2(2)+3(1)&amp;=&amp;7\\4+3&amp;=&amp;7\\7&amp;=&amp;7\checkmark\end{array}\)</para>
<para>System C</para>
<para>\(\left\{\begin{array}{c}2x+3y=5\\2x+4y=9\end{array}\right.\)</para>
<para>Use System C to answer questions 7–9.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="7" type="enumerated">
  <item> What is the value of \(x\)? </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> -3.5</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="8" type="enumerated">
  <item> What is the value of \(y\)? </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 4</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="9" type="enumerated">
  <item> Show your reasoning and check the solution. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis></para>
<para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}-1(2x+3y&amp;=&amp;5)\\-2x-3y&amp;=&amp;-5)\end{array}\)</para>

<para> \(\begin{array}{l}\underline{\begin{array}{rcl}-2x-3y&amp;=&amp;-5\\+\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;2x+4y&amp;=&amp;9\;\end{array}}\\\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;y=4\end{array}\)</para>

<para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}2x+4y&amp;=&amp;9\\2x+4(4)&amp;=&amp;9\\2x+16&amp;=&amp;9\\2x&amp;=&amp;-7\\x&amp;=&amp;-\frac72\\&amp;=&amp;-3.5\end{array}\)</para>

<para> \(\begin{array}{rcl}2x+3y&amp;=&amp;5\\2(-3.5)+3(4)&amp;=&amp;5\\-7+12&amp;=&amp;5\\5&amp;=&amp;5\checkmark\end{array}\)</para>

<para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}2x+4y&amp;=&amp;9\\2(-3.5)+4(4)&amp;=&amp;9\\-7+16&amp;=&amp;9\\9&amp;=&amp;9\checkmark\end{array}\)</para>
<para>System D</para>
<para>\(\left\{\begin{array}{c}6x+9y=48\\6x-5y=20\end{array}\right.\)</para>
<para>Use System D to answer questions 10–12.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="10" type="enumerated">
  <item> What is the value of \(x\)? </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 5</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="11" type="enumerated">
  <item> What is the value of \(y\)? </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 2</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="12" type="enumerated">
  <item> Show your reasoning and check the solution. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis></para>
<para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}-1(6x+9y&amp;=&amp;48)\\-6x-9y&amp;=&amp;-48\end{array}\)</para>

<para>\(\begin{array}{l}\underline{\begin{array}{rcl}-6x-9y&amp;=&amp;-48\\+\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;6x-5y&amp;=&amp;20\end{array}}\\\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;-14y=-28\\\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;y=2\end{array}\)</para>

<para> \(\begin{array}{rcl}6x-5y&amp;=&amp;20\\6x-5(2)&amp;=&amp;20\\6x-10&amp;=&amp;20\\6x&amp;=&amp;30\\x&amp;=&amp;5\end{array}\)</para>

<para> \(\begin{array}{rcl}6x+9y&amp;=&amp;48\\6(5)+9(2)&amp;=&amp;48\\30+18&amp;=&amp;48\\48&amp;=&amp;48\checkmark\end{array}\)</para>

<para> \(\begin{array}{rcl}6x-5y&amp;=&amp;20\\6(5)-5(2)&amp;=&amp;20\\30-10&amp;=&amp;20\\20&amp;=&amp;20\checkmark\end{array}\)</para>
<title>Student Facing Extension</title>
<title>Are you ready for more?</title>
<para>This system has three equations:</para>
<para>\(\left\{\begin{array}{c}3x+2y-z=7 \\-3x+5y+2z=-14\\3x+y-z=10\end{array}\right.\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item> Add the first two equations to get a new equation. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(7y+z=-7\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item> Add the second two equations to get a new equation. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(6y+z=-4\)</para>
<para>Solve the system of your two new equations, then answer questions 3 and 4.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item> What is the value of \(y\)? </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> -3</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item> What is the value of \(z\)? </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 14</para>
<para>Solve the original system of equations, then answer questions 5–7.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item> What is the value of \(x\)? </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 9</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="6" type="enumerated">
  <item> What is the value of \(y\)? </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> -3</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="7" type="enumerated">
  <item> What is the value of \(z\)? </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 14</para>

<title>Activity Synthesis</title>
<para>Invite students to share their solutions and strategies for the first three systems and how they check their solutions. Then, focus the discussion on the last system. Solicit the strategies students used for approaching that system. If someone solved it by substitution, display the work for all to see. If no one did, ask if it is possible to do. (If time permits, consider asking students to attempt to do so, or demonstrating that strategy to illustrate that it is not very efficient.)</para>
<para>Discuss questions such as:</para>
<list>
  <item>“What happens if we add or subtract the equations?” (No variables are eliminated, so it doesn’t help with solving.)</item>
  <item>“Why were you able to solve the first three systems by elimination but not the last one? What features were in the equations in those systems but not in the last one?” (In the first three systems, at least one variable in each pair of equations has the same or opposite coefficients, so when the terms are added or subtracted, the result is 0.)</item>
  <item>We need new moves! Tell students that they will explore another way to solve a system by elimination in an upcoming lesson.</item>
</list>
<list>
</list>

<div class="os-raise-extrasupport">
  <note>
    <para>Support for English Language Learners<title>Support for English Language Learners</title></para>
    <para>MLR 3 Clarify, Critique, Correct: Reading, Writing, Speaking<title>MLR 3 Clarify, Critique, Correct: Reading, Writing, Speaking</title></para>
  </note>
  <div class="os-raise-extrasupport-body">
    <para>Before students share their solutions and strategies, present an incorrect method of solving a linear system. For example, “After the first step when solving the system \( 2x+y=9 \) and \( x−y=3 \) by elimination, the resulting equation is \( x=12 \).” Ask students to identify the error, critique the reasoning, and write a correct explanation. As students discuss with a partner, listen for students who clarify the meaning of the solution to a system. Invite students to share their critiques and corrected explanations with the class. Listen for and amplify the language students use to describe what happens when adding two equations together and explain what the solution should be. This helps students evaluate and improve on the written mathematical arguments of others as they clarify how to find the solution of a linear system by using the elimination method.</para>
    <para class="os-raise-text-italicize">Design Principle(s): Optimize output (for explanation); Maximize meta-awareness</para>
    <para class="os-raise-extrasupport-title">Learn more about this routine</para>
    <para> <link url="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lozZJ21i3mQ;&amp;rel=0">View the instructional video</link> and <link url="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/1de908dfe7cb03fb7faad7f0277b41cf006d67a9">follow along with the materials</link> to assist you with learning this routine. </para>
    <para class="os-raise-extrasupport-title">Provide support for students</para>
    <para> <link url="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/f0d3544af9d0ff187158a6299ec77dc9d47e677e">Distribute graphic organizers</link> to the students to assist them with participating in this routine. </para>
  </div>
</div>

<title>2.5.3: Self Check</title>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold"><emphasis>After the activity, students will answer the following question to check their understanding of the concepts explored in the activity.</emphasis></para>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">QUESTION:</para>
<para>A mechanic’s garage purchased 8 brake pads and 5 headlight sets for inventory and paid $1,460. Later, the mechanic purchased another order of 6 of the same brake pads and 5 of the same headlight sets and paid $1,220. How much does each part cost?</para>
<table class="os-raise-textheavytable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Answers</entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Feedback</entry>
    </row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>brake pads: $120; headlight sets: $100</entry>
      <entry>That’s correct! Check yourself: The equations representing the constraints are \( 8b + 5h = 1,460 \) and \( 6b + 5h = 1,220 \).</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>brake pads: $100; headlight sets: $100</entry>
      <entry>Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: The equations representing the constraints are \( 8b + 5h = 1,460 \) and \( 6b + 5h = 1,220 \). Subtract the equations to get \( 2b = 240 \) and solve for \( b \). Then solve for \( h \). The answer is brake pads: $120; headlight sets: $100.</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>brake pads: $80; headlight sets: $140</entry>
      <entry>Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: The equations representing the constraints are \( 8b + 5h = 1,460 \) and \( 6b + 5h = 1,220 \). Subtract the equations to get \( 2b = 240 \) and solve for \( b \). Then solve for \( h \). The answer is brake pads: $120; headlight sets: $100.</entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>brake pads: $80; headlight sets: $148</entry>
      <entry>Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: The equations representing the constraints are \( 8b + 5h = 1,460 \) and \( 6b + 5h = 1,220 \). Subtract the equations to get \( 2b = 240 \) and solve for \( b \). Then solve for \( h \). The answer is brake pads: $120; headlight sets: $100.</entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>

<title>2.5.3: Additional Resources</title>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold"><emphasis>The following content is available to students who would like more support based on their experience with the self check. Students will not automatically have access to this content, so you may wish to share it with those who could benefit from it.</emphasis></para>
<title>Solving a System of Equations by Elimination</title>
<para>Let’s reinforce the steps to solve a system of equations using elimination.</para>

<note>
  <para><emphasis>Step 1 - </emphasis>Write both equations in standard form. If any coefficients are fractions, clear them.</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Check to see if the coefficients of one variable are opposites. If so, proceed to Step 3. If not, we will cover that in the next lesson.</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Add the equations resulting from Step 2 to eliminate one variable.</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 4 - </emphasis>Solve for the remaining variable.</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 5</emphasis> - Substitute the solution from Step 4 into one of the original equations. Then solve for the other variable.</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 6 - </emphasis>Write the solution as an ordered pair.</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 7 - </emphasis>Check that the ordered pair is a solution to both original equations.</para>
</note>

<para><emphasis>Example</emphasis></para>
<para>Let’s look at an example. Solve the following system of equations using elimination:</para>
<para>\( \left\{ \begin{array}{c l} 3x - 3y = -24 \\ -3x + 5y = 30 \end{array}\right. \)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1 </emphasis>- Write both equations in standard form. If any coefficients are fractions, clear them.
  \( \left\{ \begin{array}{c l} 3x - 3y = -24 \\ -3x + 5y = 30 \end{array}\right. \)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Check to see if the coefficients of one variable are opposites or equivalent.
  The coefficients on the \(x\)-variables are opposites. We can add the equations.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Add the equations to eliminate one variable.
  \(\begin{array}{rcl}3x-3y&amp;=&amp;-24\\-3x+5y&amp;=&amp;30\\2y&amp;=&amp;6\end{array}\) </para>
<para><emphasis>Step 4 </emphasis>- Solve for the remaining variable.
  \(\begin{array}{rcl}2y&amp;=&amp;6\\y&amp;=&amp;3\end{array}\) </para>
<para><emphasis>Step 5</emphasis> - Substitute the solution from Step 4 into one of the original equations. Then solve for the other variable.
  \begin{array}{rcl}3x-3y&amp;=&amp;-24\\3x-3(3)&amp;=&amp;-24\\3x-9&amp;=&amp;-24\\3x&amp;=&amp;-15\\x&amp;=&amp;-5\end{array}</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 6 </emphasis>- Write the solution as an ordered pair.
  \( (-5,
  3) \)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 7</emphasis> - Check that the ordered pair is a solution to both original equations.</para>
<div class="os-raise-d-flex os-raise-justify-content-center">
  <para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}3x-3y&amp;=&amp;-24\\3(-5)-3(3)&amp;=&amp;-24\\-15-9&amp;=&amp;-24\\-24&amp;=&amp;-24\checkmark\end{array}\)</para>
  <para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}-3x+5y&amp;=&amp;30\\-3(-5)+5(3)&amp;=&amp;30\\15+15&amp;=&amp;30\\30&amp;=&amp;30\checkmark\end{array}\)</para>
</div>

<title>Try It: Solving a System of Equations by Elimination</title>
<para>Solve the following system of equations using elimination:</para>
<para>\( \left\{ \begin{array}{c l} 4x - 6y = 8 \\ x + 6y = 17 \end{array}\right. \)</para>
<para>Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis></para>
<para>Here is how to solve this system of equations using elimination:</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Write both equations in standard form. If any coefficients are fractions, clear them.
  \(\begin{array}{rcl}4x-6y&amp;=&amp;8\\x+6y&amp;=&amp;17\end{array}\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Check to see if the coefficients of one variable are opposites or equivalent.
  The coefficients on the \(y\)-variables are opposites. We can add the equations.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3 </emphasis>- Add the equations to eliminate one variable.
  \(\begin{array}{rcl}4x\;-\;6y\;&amp;=&amp;\;8\\x\;+\;6y\;&amp;=&amp;\;17\\5x\;&amp;=&amp;\;25\end{array}\) </para>
<para><emphasis>Step 4 </emphasis>- Solve for the remaining variable.
  \(\begin{array}{rcl}5x\;&amp;=&amp;\;25\\x\;&amp;=&amp;\;5\end{array}\) </para>
<para><emphasis>Step 5</emphasis> - Substitute the solution from Step 4 into one of the original equations. Then solve for the other variable.
  \(\begin{array}{rcl}4x\;-\;6y\;&amp;=&amp;\;8\\\;4(5)\;-\;6y\;&amp;=&amp;\;8\\20\;-\;6y\;&amp;=&amp;\;8\\-6y\;&amp;=&amp;\;-12\\y\;&amp;=&amp;\;2\end{array}\) </para>
<para><emphasis>Step 6</emphasis> - Write the solution as an ordered pair.
  \( (5, 2) \)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 7</emphasis> - Check that the ordered pair is a solution to both original equations.</para>
<div class="os-raise-d-flex os-raise-justify-content-center">
  <para>\(\begin{array}{rcl}4x-6y&amp;=&amp;8\\4(5)-6(2)&amp;=&amp;8\\20-12&amp;=&amp;8\\8&amp;=&amp;8\checkmark\end{array}\)</para>
  <para> \(\begin{array}{rcl}x+6y&amp;=&amp;17\\(5)+6(2)&amp;=&amp;17\\5+12&amp;=&amp;17\\17&amp;=&amp;17\checkmark\end{array}\)</para>
</div>
</section>
</content>
</document>
